  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nSfc high pressure will gradually shift E of the OH Vly through\ntonight, allowing for warmer air to advect northward on the wrn \nperiphery of the departing high. Slightly higher PWs will also evolve\ninto the region midweek as deep-layer SW flow gradually increases.\nBoth overnight and daytime temperatures will continue to nudge upward\nthrough Thursday, with highs reaching into the lower to mid 80s\nWednesday and upper 80s to near 90 degrees on Thursday. \n\nAlthough conditions may not be /quite/ as favorable tonight as has\nbeen the case the past several nights, patchy to areas of river \nvalley fog should again develop, particularly along the larger\nwaterways. Some patchy fog can also be expected in parts of central\nto south-central OH where the skies will remain the clearest and wind\nwill be the calmest.\n\nMidlevel ridging will expand across the OH/TN Vlys Wednesday into\nThursday before dampening with the approach of a trof by Friday.\nMid/high level clouds will begin to overspread the region through\nthis time well ahead of any rain chances. The trof will flatten with\neastward extent, becoming less pronounced into late Friday and \nFriday night. Even so, expect a band of light rain to overspread the\narea by Friday afternoon into Friday night from NW to SE, coincident\nwith the frontal passage. Friday may end up being a somewhat soggy \nday across most of the area, even with relatively modest rain amounts\n(generally one quarter to one half inch). With this, highs on Friday\nwill be substantially cooler than will be the case Thursday, with \nthe drier and cooler air filtering in by Saturday into Sunday. \n\nThere are still some uncertainties regarding the development of a \nweak wave along the front Saturday, which may cause the front to slow\nits southward progression or perhaps stall, potentially allowing the\nrain to linger a bit longer across far S/SE parts of the local area.\nShould this scenario evolve, rain may linger near/south of the OH \nRvr into Saturday/Saturday night as well. However, confidence remains\nhigh in a return to drier conditions for most, if not all, of the \nILN FA by Saturday afternoon through the remainder of the weekend. \n\nKey Message 2)\nConfidence is high in a return to dry conditions by early next week,\nwith below normal temperatures likely Sunday through Tuesday.\nDepending on the exact setup, some very patchy frost cannot be\ncompletely ruled out Sunday night and especially Monday night, but \nconfidence in this scenario is low at this juncture. Ensemble probs \nof temps at or below 38F are about 10-20% Monday night, particularly \nnear/north of I-70.",
